WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.888
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:11.630
So this example has
some serious effects

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.760
for production sized
apps, where you

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.270
have authentication or things
that testing should not

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.700
be confirming.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.920
One of the difficulties
when doing production size

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.810
apps and you're running
it through unit tests

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.450
is interacting with
outside sources.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.850
This can be authentication.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.710
This can be database requests.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.810
And one of the things
that we recommend you do

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:37.610
is you listen to a
flag or a global option

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.830
of shiny.testmode.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.160
And if shiny.testmode
is true, I recommend

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.610
having a different UI,
or server, or reactive

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.160
behavior given this flag.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.950
And you can have things
like the authentication

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.780
isn't even addressed.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.640
If I do a restricted database
call, maybe instead I

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.800
load a CSV of information.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.830
One, we're not
testing the database.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.580
We're actually just
testing the Shiny app.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.390
So we need to try to isolate
our test to what we can control.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:12.290
And using this flag, we can
turn that behavior on or turn it

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:12.990
off.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.880
And I think it's just really
great as how you can override

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.530
your apps' default behavior.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.860
So let's look at
the app that we've

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.980
been doing in the past two
videos where we ask our name,

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.250
and it'll tell us
the first part,

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.720
or first letter of our name.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.560
And then let's just add in
a third field of penguins.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.750
And so to lighten the
mood a little bit,

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.170
we're going to say
how many penguins are

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.250
going to be coming for dinner.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.050
And so let's just add
this into our app.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.080
And we have it here.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.740
I'm just going to run it
for demonstration purposes.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:49.973
And I click Greet.

00:01:49.973 --> 00:01:50.890
It said, Hello Barret.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.348
The first letter
in your name is b.

00:01:52.348 --> 00:01:56.570
And there are 333 penguins
coming for dinner.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.670
And this is fun.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.180
But if I keep
hitting Greet, if we

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.860
look as to where this
penguin count is coming from,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.100
it's coming from this
authenticated database

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.840
request, or something
that I'm going

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.330
to have returned random data.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:17.360
And so if I click Greet
again, now it's 330, 324, 336.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.430
This is not
deterministic at all.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.973
And this is, therefore,
not useful for testing.

00:02:21.973 --> 00:02:24.140
Because if you keep running
this test over and over,

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:25.130
it will fail.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.650
There will be no way
for it to be correct,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.110
because the data
changes underneath.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.970
So instead, we can
listen to our flag.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:39.020
So I'm going to change,
just wrap this one part

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:45.860
and say if we'll get an
option of shiny.testmode,

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.090
we'll default this
value to false.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:54.570
But if it's true, then I want to
return a test database request.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.230
So this is something
that returns static data,

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.090
and will only retrieve
what I want it to have.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.160
Otherwise, if I'm
not in test mode,

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.630
then let's just do the
authenticated database request.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.210
This will be stored
into penguin data,

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.300
and then the penguin data
will count the number of rows,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.480
and then hopefully we
have a consistent count.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.330
Now, if I just run my
app and reload app,

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.110
this is not in testing mode.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.600
So we should still see
our original behavior.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.820
So I see Barret.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:26.540
We see Greet.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:32.330
It's something like 333, or 321,
different amount of each time

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:33.890
when I click Greet.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:41.430
But let's run this just looking
at our application here.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.610
So if I run this with the
app, and I call app$view,

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:52.400
so that I have it, it opens up
into chromium based browser,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.720
and I have What's your name.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:55.610
We call set input to Barret.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.330
We call app Greet.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.730
We can see that updated.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.247
And here it says, Hello Barret.

00:04:00.247 --> 00:04:01.580
The first letter your name is b.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:05.270
And there are 68 penguins
coming for dinner.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.980
It's a funny
phrase, but it's 68.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.240
It's not 300.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.900
And so if I click Greet
again, and again, and again,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.020
and again, 68 is now constant.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.720
And it's still
going to stay there.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:23.870
If I look at the values to see
what we have, app$get_values,

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:27.020
I have output penguins,
there are 38 penguins coming

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.040
for dinner.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:30.650
I could actually
update that, so that we

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:36.120
have another exported test
value of the penguin count.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.440
So if I update with
the penguin count,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.630
say penguin_count = a
reactive expression,

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.790
where I export
that penguin count.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:52.130
Save it, and then I'm going
to stop this app here.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.750
And then I'm just
going to restart

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:58.625
those interactive tests, where
I run app, and I click Greet.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.050
And let's view it.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.793
Now, I don't need to
necessarily open up

00:05:04.793 --> 00:05:05.960
the viewer at the beginning.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.690
It's usually useful
when debugging errors.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:11.000
But it's still says 68.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:20.090
And app$get_value of export
= penguin_count is 68.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:20.810
Perfect.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.150
So now we can make
assertions on this value,

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:26.060
and it'll be consistent
every single time.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.520
This is just so useful for
getting around those database

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.270
calls that take 5, 10
minutes to execute,

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.450
or you're asking a different
API about something that is

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.100
outside the control of Shiny.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.220
We want these unit tests
to be consistent and fast.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.581
That way you want to do them,
and you want to do them often.

00:05:43.581 --> 00:05:46.635
[MUSIC PLAYING]

